ion of the diagonals, which is also the midpoint between the vertices. The distance from the centroid to each edge isn't the same for all edges. For example, the distance from the centroid to a side of length 10 m and the distance to a side of length 8 m will be different.\n\nSo, let me compute the distance from the centroid to each of the four sides. In a parallelogram, the distance from the center to each side is half the height of the parallelogram. Wait, but since the centroid is the midpoint of the diagonals, maybe we can compute these distances.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider the coordinates. Maybe assign coordinates to the vertices of the base to compute these distances more easily.\n\nLet me try setting up a coordinate system. Let's place the centroid at the origin (0, 0, 0). Since the pyramid's height is 4 m, the apex is at (0, 0, 4). Now, the base is a parallelogram with sides 10 m and 8 m, and a diagonal of 6 m. Let me try to define the coordinates of the four vertices of the base.\n\nBut maybe this will complicate things. Alternatively, since the centroid is the intersection of the diagonals, each diagonal is split into two equal parts. The diagonals are 6 m and \u221a292 \u2248 17.09 m as calculated earlier. Wait, but if one diagonal is 6 m, then each half is 3 m, and the other diagonal is \u221a292, so each half is \u221a292 / 2 \u2248 8.544 m.\n\nBut maybe there's a better way. Let me recall that in a parallelogram, the area is also equal to the product of the length of a diagonal and the distance from the center to the opposite side. Wait, not exactly. Alternatively, the area can be expressed as the product of the side length and the corresponding height. Since the area is 48 m\u00b2, the heights corresponding to the sides of 10 m and 8 m would be 48/10 = 4.8 m and 48/8 = 6 m. So, the height corresponding to the side of 10 m is 4.8 m, and the height corresponding to the side of 8 m is 6 m.\n\nWait, that makes sense. In a parallelogram, the area is 