rhaps it's better to assign coordinates. Let me try:\n\nLet\u2019s place the centroid at (0,0,0). Let\u2019s assume diagonal AC is along the x-axis. So point A is (-3, 0, 0), point C is (3, 0, 0). The other diagonal BD is of length sqrt(292) \u224817.09 m, so from centroid to B and D is sqrt(292)/2 \u22488.5445 m. Let\u2019s denote the coordinates of point B as (0, y, 0) and point D as (0, -y, 0), since BD is perpendicular to AC? Wait, no. Wait, in a parallelogram, diagonals are not necessarily perpendicular unless it's a rhombus. Wait, but in general, the diagonals can be at any angle.\n\nBut since we have sides AB =10 m, BC=8 m. Let\u2019s find coordinates of points B and D.\n\nWait, if points A is (-3, 0, 0) and C is (3, 0, 0), then vector AC is (6, 0, 0). The other diagonal BD has points B and D such that B is (p, q, 0) and D is (-p, -q, 0). The midpoint of BD is (0,0,0), which is the centroid.\n\nNow, the sides AB and BC. The length AB is 10 m. AB is from A(-3,0,0) to B(p,q,0). So vector AB is (p + 3, q, 0). The magnitude is sqrt[(p + 3)^2 + q^2] = 10.\n\nSimilarly, BC is from B(p, q, 0) to C(3,0,0). Vector BC is (3 - p, -q, 0). The magnitude is sqrt[(3 - p)^2 + q^2] = 8.\n\nSo we have two equations:\n\n1. (p + 3)^2 + q^2 = 100\n\n2. (3 - p)^2 + q^2 = 64\n\nSubtract equation 2 from equation 1:\n\n(p + 3)^2 - (3 - p)^2 = 100 - 64\n\nExpanding both squares:\n\n[(p^2 + 6p + 9) - (p^2 - 6p + 9)] = 36\n\nSimplify:\n\n(p^2 +6p +9 - p^2 +6p -9) = 12p = 36\n\nTherefore, 12p = 36 \u2192 p = 3.\n\nPlugging back p = 3 into equation 1:\n\n(3 + 3)^2 + q^2 = 100 \u2192 6^2 + q^2 = 100 \u2192 36 + q^2 = 100 \u2192 q^2 = 64 \u2192 q = \u00b18.\n\nTherefore, coordinates of B are (3, 8, 0) or (3, -8, 0). Let's take q = 8, so B is (3, 8, 0). Then D, being (-p, -q, 0) = (-3, -8, 0).\n\nWait, but let's check the length of BC with p =3, q=8.\n\nFrom B(3,8,0) to C(3,0,0): the vector is (0, -8, 0), so length is 8 m, which matches. And AB is from A(-3,0,0) to B(3,8,0): vector is (6,8,0