s for the triangular faces are:\n   - For sides of 10 m: \\(\\sqrt{4^2 + 2.4^2} = \\sqrt{21.76} = 4\\sqrt{34}/5\\)\n   - For sides of 8 m: \\(\\sqrt{4^2 + 3^2} = 5 \\, \\text{m}\\)\n\nThe lateral surface areas are calculated as:\n   - For sides of 10 m: \\(2 \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 4\\sqrt{34}/5\\right) = 8\\sqrt{34}\\)\n   - For sides of 8 m: \\(2 \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 8 \\times 5\\right) = 40\\)\n\nSumming the lateral surface areas and adding the base area:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Surface Area} = 48 + 8\\sqrt{34} + 40 = 88 + 8\\sqrt{34}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the total surface area of the pyramid is \\(\\boxed{88 + 8\\sqrt{34}}\\) square meters.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Given Data:**\n   - The base of the pyramid is a parallelogram with side lengths \\( AB = 8 \\ \\text{m} \\) and \\( AD = 10 \\ \\text{m} \\).\n   - One of the diagonals, \\( BD \\), is given as \\( 6 \\ \\text{m} \\).\n   - The height of the pyramid passing through the intersection point of the diagonals of the base is \\( 4 \\ \\text{m} \\).\n\n2. **Identify Right Triangle:**\n   Given: \\( AB = 8 \\ \\text{m} \\), \\( AD = 10 \\ \\text{m} \\), and \\( BD = 6 \\ \\text{m} \\). By the Pythagorean theorem:\n   \\[\n   6^2 + 8^2 = 10^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   36 + 64 = 100\n   \\]\n   Hence, \\( \\triangle ABD \\) is a right triangle with \\( \\angle ABD = 90^\\circ \\). \n\n3. **Area of the Base:**\n   Since \\( \\triangle ABD \\) (one half of the parallelogram) is right with legs \\( AB = 8 \\ \\text{m} \\) and \\( BD = 6 \\ \\text{m} \\), the area of the entire parallelogram (base) is:\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{base}} = 8 \\times 6 = 48 \\ \\text{m}^2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Area of Triangular Faces (using height and Pythagorean theorem):**\n   - For \\(\\triangle ASB\\), where \\( S \\) is the apex.\n     Given \\( AB = 8 \\ \\text{m} \\), height \\( SO = 4 \\ \\text{m} \\), and \\( OB = \\frac{BD}{2} = 3 \\ \\text{m} \\):\n     \\[\n     BS = \\sqrt{SO^2 + OB^2} = \\sqrt{4^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{16 + 9} = \\sqrt{25} = 5 \\ \\text{m}\n     \\]\n     Area:\n     \\[\n     S_{\\triangle ASB} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times AB \\times BS = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8 \\times 5 = 20 \\ \\text{m}^2\n     \\]\n\n5. **Height to Side \\(AD\\) Analysis and Area:**\n   - For \\(\\triangle ASD\\):\n     We need to find the length \\( SK \\), where \\( K \\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\( S \\) to \\( AD \\).\n     Using similarity of triangles \\( \\triangle OKD \\) and \\( \\triangle ABD \\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{OK}{AB} = \\frac{OD}{AD}, \\quad OD = \\frac{AD}{2} = 3 \\ \\text{m}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     OK = \\frac{8 \\times 3}{10} = \\frac{24}{10} = \\frac{12}{5} \\ \\text{m}\n     \\]\n     Length \\( SK \\):\n     \\[\n     SK = \\sqrt{SO^2 + OK^2} = \\sqrt{4^2 + \\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{16 + \\frac{144}{25}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{400}{25}} + \\frac{144}{25} = \\frac{4 \\sqrt{34}}{5} \\ \\text{m}\n     \\]\n\n     Area:\n     \\[\n     S_{\\triangle ASD} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times AD \\times SK = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times \\frac{4 \\sqrt{34}}{5} = 4 \\sqrt{34} \\ \\text{m}^2\n     \\]\n\n6. **Total Surface Area:**\n   Adding up the areas from each face:\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{total}} = S_{\\text{base}} + 2 \\times S_{\\triangle ASB} + 2 \\times S_{\\triangle ASD}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the values:\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{total}} = 48 + 2 \\times 20 + 2 \\times 4 \\sqrt{34}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{total}} = 48 + 40 + 8 \\sqrt{34}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{total}} = 88 + 8 \\sqrt{34}\n   \\]\n\n   Conclusion: \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{8(11 + \\sqrt{34}) \\ \\text{m}^2}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A parallelepiped \\( ABCD A_{1} B_{1} C_{1} D_{1} \\) is given. A point \\( X \\) is chosen on edge \\( A_{1} D_{1} \\) and a point \\( Y \\) is chosen on edge \\( BC \\). It is known that \\( A_{1}X = 5 \\), \\( BY = 3 \\), and \\( B_{1} C_{1} = 14 \\). 