tute \u03bc = 1:\n\n(c + d\u2082) - c \u03bb - d\u2082 = 0 \u21d2 c + d\u2082 - d\u2082 - c \u03bb = 0 \u21d2 c(1 - \u03bb) = 0\n\nAssuming c \u2260 0 (since BC is 14, which is along the y-axis? Wait, no. Wait, in our coordinate system, BC is along the x-axis from (14, c, 0) to (0, c, 0). So c is the y-coordinate for points B and C. If c were 0, then points B and C would be on the x-axis, making ABCD a line segment, which is degenerate. So c \u2260 0.\n\nTherefore, 1 - \u03bb = 0 \u21d2 \u03bb = 1.\n\nNow, substitute \u03bb = 1 and \u03bc = 1 into the x-coordinate equation:\n\nx = d\u2081 + 1*9 + 1*(11 - d\u2081) = d\u2081 + 9 + 11 - d\u2081 = 20\n\nTherefore, x = 20, which is on DA ray at (20, 0, 0). Therefore, s = 20/14 = 10/7, so DZ = 20. Same result as before.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 20.\n\nThis seems consistent across two different methods: solving the plane equation and using parametric equations for the plane. Both lead to DZ = 20.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\boxed{20}\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{20}", "deepseek_solution": "Given a parallelepiped \\(ABCD A_1 B_1 C_1 D_1\\), we need to find the length \\(DZ\\) where point \\(Z\\) is the intersection of the plane \\(C_1XY\\) with the ray \\(DA\\). \n\n1. **Coordinates Assignment**:\n   - Place \\(D\\) at the origin \\((0, 0, 0)\\).\n   - \\(A\\) is at \\((14, 0, 0)\\) since \\(B_1C_1 = 14\\) implies \\(a = 14\\).\n   - \\(B\\) is at \\((14, c, 0)\\), \\(C\\) is at \\((0, c, 0)\\), and \\(D_1\\) is at \\((d_1, d_2, d_3)\\).\n\n2. **Points Coordinates**:\n   - \\(A_1\\) is \\((14 + d_1, d_2, d_3)\\).\n   - \\(C_1\\) is \\((d_1, c + d_2, d_3)\\).\n   - \\(X\\) is on \\(A_1D_1\\) such that \\(A_1X = 5\\), so \\(X = (9 + d_1, d_2, d_3)\\).\n   - \\(Y\\) is on \\(BC\\) such that \\(BY = 3\\), so \\(Y = (11, c, 0)\\).\n\n3. **Plane Equation**:\n   - Vectors \\(C_1X = (9, -c, 0)\\) and \\(C_1Y = (11 - d_1, -d_2, -d_3)\\).\n   - Normal vector \\(N = C_1X \\times C_1Y = (c d_3, 9 d_3, -9 d_2 + 11c - c d_1)\\