dinates of \\(C\\) are derived as \\(\\left(\\frac{a\\sqrt{d^2 - a^2}}{d}, \\frac{a^2}{d}\\right)\\). Point \\(K\\) is on \\(AD\\) such that \\(AK = AC = a\\), so \\(K\\) is \\((0,a)\\).\n\nNext, we find the angle bisector of \\(\\angle ADC\\). Using vector analysis and parametric equations, we determine the angle bisector intersects line \\(BK\\) (which has equation \\(y = -x + a\\)). Solving the intersection, we find coordinates of \\(M\\).\n\nFor simplification, assume \\(d = a\\sqrt{2}\\). This choice simplifies \\(C\\) to \\(\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2}}, \\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\). The angle bisector of \\(\\angle ADC\\) (now a 45\u00b0 angle) bisects to 22.5\u00b0, leading to a slope of \\(-(\\sqrt{2} + 1)\\). Solving for intersection \\(M\\), we find \\(M\\) at \\(\\left(a\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right), \\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\).\n\nVectors \\(\\vec{CA}\\) and \\(\\vec{CM}\\) are computed, and the angle between them is found using the dot product. Calculations yield \\(\\cos\\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\), so \\(\\theta = 45^\\circ\\).\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{45^\\circ}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven:\n1. In quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\), side \\(AB\\) is equal to diagonal \\(AC\\).\n2. \\(AB \\perp AD\\) and \\(AC \\perp CD\\).\n3. Point \\(K\\) is on \\(AD\\) such that \\(AC = AK\\).\n4. The bisector of \\(\\angle ADC\\) intersects \\(BK\\) at point \\(M\\).\n\nSteps to find \\(\\angle ACM\\):\n\n1. **Identify Right-Angle and Isosceles Triangle:**\n   - Triangle \\(BAK\\) is right-angled at \\(A\\).\n   - \\(AB = AC\\) given, so \\(BAK\\) is also isosceles with \\(AB = AK\\).\n   - Thus, \\(\\angle AKB = \\angle BAK = 45^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Analyzing Bisector Intersection:**\n   - The bisector of \\(\\angle CAD = \\angle DAC\\) intersects segment \\(BK\\) at point \\(N\\).\n   - Triangles \\(ANK\\) and \\(ANC\\) are congruent since:\n     - \\(AN\\) is a common side.\n     - \\(AK = AC\\).\n     - \\(\\angle CAN = \\angle KAN\\).\n\