 x1 + m y1 + n z1 = p\n\nFor P2: l x2 + m y2 + n z2 = p\n\nFor P3: l x3 + m y3 + n z3 = p\n\nFor P4: l x4 + m y4 + n z4 = p\n\nExpressed in terms of the coordinates:\n\nl (h\u00b2/(a D1)) + m (h\u00b2/(b D1)) + n (h / D1) = p\n\nl (-h\u00b2/(c D2)) + m (h\u00b2/(b D2)) + n (h / D2) = p\n\nl (-h\u00b2/(c D3)) + m (-h\u00b2/(d D3)) + n (h / D3) = p\n\nl (h\u00b2/(a D4)) + m (-h\u00b2/(d D4)) + n (h / D4) = p\n\nMultiply each equation by D1, D2, D3, D4 respectively:\n\nl h\u00b2/a + m h\u00b2/b + n h = p D1 ...(1)\n\n- l h\u00b2/c + m h\u00b2/b + n h = p D2 ...(2)\n\n- l h\u00b2/c - m h\u00b2/d + n h = p D3 ...(3)\n\nl h\u00b2/a - m h\u00b2/d + n h = p D4 ...(4)\n\nNow, subtract equation (1) - equation (2):\n\n[l h\u00b2/a + m h\u00b2/b + n h] - [-l h\u00b2/c + m h\u00b2/b + n h] = p (D1 - D2)\n\nLeft side:\n\nl h\u00b2/a + l h\u00b2/c = l h\u00b2 (1/a + 1/c )\n\nRight side:\n\np (D1 - D2) = p [ (h\u00b2/a\u00b2 + h\u00b2/b\u00b2 + 1 ) - (h\u00b2/c\u00b2 + h\u00b2/b\u00b2 + 1 ) ] = p (h\u00b2/a\u00b2 - h\u00b2/c\u00b2 )\n\nThus:\n\nl h\u00b2 (1/a + 1/c ) = p (h\u00b2/a\u00b2 - h\u00b2/c\u00b2 )\n\nSimilarly, equation (2) - equation (3):\n\n[-l h\u00b2/c + m h\u00b2/b + n h] - [-l h\u00b2/c - m h\u00b2/d + n h] = p (D2 - D3)\n\nLeft side:\n\nm h\u00b2/b + m h\u00b2/d = m h\u00b2 (1/b + 1/d )\n\nRight side:\n\np [ (h\u00b2/c\u00b2 + h\u00b2/b\u00b2 + 1 ) - (h\u00b2/c\u00b2 + h\u00b2/d\u00b2 + 1 ) ] = p (h\u00b2/b\u00b2 - h\u00b2/d\u00b2 )\n\nThus:\n\nm h\u00b2 (1/b + 1/d ) = p (h\u00b2/b\u00b2 - h\u00b2/d\u00b2 )\n\nSimilarly, equation (3) - equation (4):\n\n[-l h\u00b2/c - m h\u00b2/d + n h] - [l h\u00b2/a - m h\u00b2/d + n h] = p (D3 - D4 )\n\nLeft side:\n\n- l h\u00b2/c - l h\u00b2/a = -l h\u00b2 (1/c + 1/a )\n\nRight side:\n\np [ (h\u00b2/c\u00b2 + h\u00b2/d\u00b2 + 1 ) - (h\u00b2/a\u00b2 + h\u00b2/d\u00b2 + 1 ) ] = p (h\u00b2/c\u00b2 - h\u00b2/a\u00b2 )\n\nThus:\n\n- l h\u00b2 (1/c + 1/a ) = p (h\u00b2/c\u00b2 - h\u00b2/a\u00b2 )\n\nNotice that equations (1)-(2), (2)-(3), (3)-(4) give us three equations:\n\n1. l (1/a + 1/c ) = p (1/a\u00b2 - 1/c\u00b2 )\n\n2. m (1/b + 1/d ) = p (1/b\u00b2 - 1/d\u00b2 )\n\n3. -l (1/a + 1/c ) = p (1/c\u00b2 - 1/a\u00b2 )\n\nBut equation 3 is just negative of equation 1:\n\nFrom equation 1: l (1/a + 1/c ) = p ( (1/a - 1/c )(1/a + 1/c ) ) \u21d2 l = p (1/a - 1/c )\n\nSimilarly, equation 2:\n\nm (1/b + 1/d ) = p (1/b\u00b2 - 1/d\u00b2 ) = p (1/b - 1/d )(1/b + 1/d ) \u21d2 m = p (1/b - 1/d )\n\nEquation 3: -l (1/a + 1/c ) = p (1/c\u00b2 - 1/a\u00b2 ) = p ( (1/c - 1/a )(1/c + 1/a ) )\n\nWhich is equivalent to -l = p (1/c - 1/a ), which is the same as l = p (1/a - 1/c ), matching equation 1.\n\nSimilarly, let's solve for l and m in terms of p:\n\nl = p (1/a - 1/c )\n\nm = p (1/b - 1/d )\n\nNow, substitute l and m into equation (1):\n\nEquation (1): l h\u00b2/a + m h\u00b2/b + n h = p D1\n\nSubstitute l and m:\n\np (1/a - 1/c ) h\u00b2/a + p (1/b - 1/d ) h\u00b2/b + n h = p (h\u00b2/a\u00b2 + h\u00b2/b\u00b2 + 1 )\n\nFactor out p:\n\np [ (1/a - 1/c ) h\u00b2/a + (1/b - 1/d ) h\u00b2/b ] + n h = p (h\u00b2/a\u00b2 + h\u00b2/b\u00b2 + 1 )\n\nDivide both sides by p (assuming p \u2260 0):\n\n(1/a - 1/c ) h\u00b2/a + (1/b - 1/d ) h\u00b2/b + n h / p = h\u00b2/a\u00b2 + h\u00b2/b\u00b2 + 1 \n\nLet\u2019s compute the terms:\n\nFirst term: (1/a - 1/c ) h\u00b2/a = h\u00b2/a\u00b2 - h\u00b2/(a c )\n\nSecond term: (1/b - 1/d ) h\u00b2/b = h\u00b2/b\u00b2 - h\u00b2/(b d )\n\nSo,\n\nLeft side:\n\nh\u00b2/a\u00b2 - h\u00b2/(a c ) + h\u00b2/b\u00b2 - h\u00b2/(b d ) + n h / p = h\u00b2/a\u00b2 + h\u00b2/b\u00b2 + 1 \n\nSimplify:\n\n- h\u00b2/(a c ) - h\u00b2/(b d ) + n h / p = 1 \n\nThus,\n\nn h / p = 1 + h\u00b2/(a c ) + h\u00b2/(b d )\n\n=> n = p (1 + h\u00b2/(a c ) + h\u00b2/(b d )) / h \n\nSimilarly, we can use another equation to solve for n. Let's take equation (1):\n\nBut maybe instead, let's substitute l and m into equation (4):\n\nEquation (4): l h\u00b2/a - m h\u00b2/d + n h = p D4\n\nSubstitute l = p (1/a - 1/c ), m = p (1/b - 1/d ):\n\np (1/a - 1/c ) h\u00b2/a - p (1/b - 1/d ) h\u00b2/d + n h = p (h\u00b2/a\u00b2 + h\u00b2/d\u00b2 + 1 )\n\nFactor out p:\n\np [ (1/a - 1/c ) h\u00b2/a - (1/b - 1/d ) h\u00b2/d ] + n h = p (h\u00b2/a\u00b2 + h\u00b2/d\u00b2 + 1 )\n\nDivide by p:\n\n(1/a - 1/c ) h\u00b2/a - (1/b - 1/d ) h\u00b2/d + n h / p = h\u00b2/a\u00b2 + h\u00b2/d\u00b2 + 1 \n\nCompute the terms:\n\nFirst term: (1/a - 1/c ) h\u00b2/a = h\u00b2/a\u00b2 - h\u00b2/(a c )\n\nSecond term: - (1/b - 1/d ) h\u00b2/d = - h\u00b2/(b d ) + h\u00b2/d\u00b2\n\nSo,\n\nLeft side:\n\nh\u00b2/a\u00b2 - h\u00b2/(a c ) - h\u00b2/(b d ) + h\u00b2/d\u00b2 + n h / p = h\u00b2/a\u00b2 + h\u00b2/d\u00b2 + 1 \n\nSimplify:\n\n- h\u00b2/(a c ) - h\u00b2/(b d ) + n h / p = 1 \n\nWhich is the same as before. So, the same expression for n:\n\nn = p (1 + h\u00b2/(a c ) + h\u00b2/(b d )) / h \n\nThus, we have:\n\nl = p (1/a - 1/c )\n\nm = p (1/b - 1/d )\n\nn = p (1/h + h/(a c ) + h/(b d ) )\n\nTherefore, the plane equation is:\n\nl x + m y + n z = p\n\nSubstituting l, m, n:\n\np (1/a - 1/c ) x + p (1/b - 1/d ) y + p (1/h + h/(a c ) + h/(b d ) ) z = p \n\nDivide both sides by p (assuming p \u2260 0):\n\n(1/a - 1/c ) x + (1/b - 1/d ) y + (1/h + h/(a c ) + h/(b d ) ) z = 1 \n\nThis is the equation of the plane that the four projections lie on. Therefore, the four points P1, P2, P3, P4 are coplanar, and since they also lie on the sphere, their intersection is a circle. Hence, the projections lie on a circle.\n\nTherefore, the proof is complete.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that the projections of point \\( O \\) onto each lateral face of the quadrilateral pyramid \\( S-ABCD \\) lie on the same circle, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1: Coordinate System Setup**\n- Place \\( O \\), the intersection of perpendicular diagonals \\( AC \\) and \\( BD \\), at the origin \\((0,0,0)\\).\n- Align \\( AC \\) along the x-axis and \\( BD \\) along the y-axis. Assign coordinates:\n  - \\( A = (a, 0, 0) \\), \\( C = (-c, 0, 0) \\)\n  - \\( B = (0, b, 0) \\), \\( D = (0, -d, 0) \\)\n- Vertex \\( S \\) projects orthogonally to \\( O \\), so \\( S = (0, 0, h) \\).\n\n**Step 2: Equations of Lateral Faces**\nEach lateral face is a plane. Derive their equations using points \\( S \\), \\( A \\), \\( B \\), \\( C \\), \\( D \\):\n- **Face \\( SAB \\):** \\(\\frac{h}{a}x + \\frac{h}{b}y + z = h\\)\n- **Face \\( SBC \\):** \\(-\\frac{h}{c}x + \\frac{h}{b}y + z = h\\)\n- **Face \\( SCD \\):** \\(-\\frac{h}{c}x - \\frac{h}{d}y + z = h\\)\n- **Face \\( SDA \\):** \\(\\frac{h}{a}x - \\frac{h}{d}y + z = h\\)\n\n**Step 3: Projections of \\( O \\) onto Lateral Faces**\nThe projection of \\( O \\) onto a plane \\( Ax + By + Cz + D = 0 \\) is given by:\n\\[\nP' = -\\frac{D}{A^2 + B^2 + C^2}(A, B, C)\n\\]\nCalculate each projection:\n- **Projection \\( P_1 \\) onto \\( SAB \\):**\n  \\[\n  P_1 = \\left( \\frac{h^2/a}{D_1}, \\frac{h^2/b}{D_1}, \\frac{h}{D_1} \\right), \\quad D_1 = \\frac{h^2}{a^2} + \\frac{h^2}{b^2} + 1\n  \\]\n- **Projection \\( P_2 \\) onto \\( SBC \\):**\n  \\[\n  P_2 = \\left( -\\frac{h^2/c}{D_2}, \\frac{h^2/b}{D_2}, \\frac{h}{D_2} \\right), \\quad D_2 = \\frac{h^2}{c^2} + \\frac{h^2}{b^2} + 1\n  \\]\n- **Projection \\( P_3 \\) onto \\( SCD \\):**\n  \\[\n  P_3 = \\left( -\\frac{h^2/c}{D_3}, -\\frac{h^2/d}{D_3}, \\frac{h}{D_3} \\right), \\quad D_3 = \\frac{h^2}{c^2} + \\frac{h^2}{d^2} + 1\n  \\]\n- **Projection \\( P_4 \\) onto \\( SDA \\):**\n  \\[\n  P_4 = \\left( \\frac{h^2/a}{D_4}, -\\frac{h^2/d}{D_4}, \\frac{h}{D_4} \\right), \\quad D_4 = \\frac{h^2}{a^2} + \\frac{h^2}{d^2} + 1\n  \\]\n\n**Step 4: Coplanarity and Circle Formation**\nAll projections lie on the sphere \\( x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - hz = 0 \\). To show they lie on a circle, verify they are coplanar:\n- Derive the plane equation \\( lx + my + nz = p \\) by solving the system of equations from the projections.\n- The derived plane is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{a} - \\frac{1}{c} \\right)x + \\left( \\frac{1}{b} - \\frac{1}{d} \\right)y + \\left( \\frac{1}{h} + \\frac{h}{ac} + \\frac{h}{bd} \\right)z = 1\n\\]\n- Since all projections satisfy this plane and the sphere, their intersection is a circle.\n\n**Conclusion**\nThe projections of \\( O \\) onto the lateral faces are coplanar and lie on the intersection of a sphere and a plane, hence forming a circle. Therefore, the four projections lie on the same circle. \n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{The projections of } O \\text{ onto each lateral face lie on a common circle.}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understanding the Geometry Configuration:** \n    - We start by noting that we have a tetrahedron \\( S-ABCD \\) with base \\( ABCD \\) being a convex quadrilateral where \\( AC \\perp BD \\), and \\( O \\) is the point of intersection of \\( AC \\) and \\( BD \\). Additionally, \\( O \\) is the orthogonal projection of the vertex \\( S \\) onto the base \\( ABCD \\).\n\n2. **Projection of \\( O \\) onto Faces:** \n    - Let \\( K, L, M, N \\) be the projections of point \\( O \\) onto the faces \\( SAB, SBC, SCD, SDA \\) respectively.\n\n3. **Perpendicularity in Tetrahedron Faces:**\n    - Since \\( O \\) is the orthogonal projection of \\( S \\) onto \\( ABCD \\), the line segment \\( OS \\perp ABCD \\). Given \\( OC \\perp SD \\) and \\( OD \\perp SC \\), it follows that \\( OC \\perp SD \\) as \\( OC \\) is perpendicular to both \\( OS \\) and \\( OD \\).\n\n4. **Altitude and Centroid Relationship:**\n    - Since \\( OM \\perp SCD \\), \\( CM \\) being the projection of \\( OC \\) in \\( SCD \\), making \\( SD \\perp CM \\) and \\( DM \\perp SC \\). Therefore, \\( M \\) is the orthocenter of triangle \\( SCD \\). Similarly, points \\( K \\) and \\( L \\) are the orthocenters of the respective triangular faces they lie in.\n\n5. **Utilizing Perpendicular Lines:**\n    - Drawing \\( PO \\), we use the properties of perpendiculars in a tetrahedron. Given that the orthocenter properties hold, we conclude that \\( OP \\perp SD \\) and similar perpendicular relationships in other faces give corresponding orthocenters.\n\n6. **Intersection Relationship:**\n    - Let \\( A, P, C \\) form triangle \\( APC \\) with \\( PO \\perp AC \\), \\( ON \\perp AP \\), and \\( OM \\perp PC \\). Using triangle properties, we set coordinates: \\( OA = a, OC = b, OP = c \\).\n\n7. **Proportionality of Segments:**\n    - Using Pythagorean properties and segment proportions, we get:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{PN}{NA} = \\frac{OA^2}{OP^2} = \\frac{a^2}{c^2}, \\quad \\frac{CM}{MP} = \\frac{b^2}{c^2}.\n    \\]\n    Similarly, for another segment \\( OQ = d \\) where:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{AK}{KQ} = \\frac{a^2}{d^2}, \\quad \\frac{QL}{LC} = \\frac{d^2}{b^2}.\n    \\]\n\n8. **Validation Using Menelaus' Theorem:**\n    - Points \\( K, L, M, N \\) lie on the polymorphic shape formed by segments \\( AQ, QC, CP, PA \\) and maintain equality:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{AK}{KQ} \\cdot \\frac{QL}{LC} \\cdot \\frac{CM}{MP} \\cdot \\frac{PN}{NH} = \\frac{a^2}{d^2} \\cdot \\frac{d^2}{b^2} \\cdot \\frac{b^2}{c^2} \\cdot \\frac{c^2}{d^2} = 1.\n    \\]\n    This proves \\( K, L, M, N \\) lie on a common plane \\( \\alpha \\).\n\n9. **Sphere and Circle Conclusion:**\n    - Considering \\( SO \\) as a sphere's radius, angles \\( \\angle SKO = \\angle SLO = \\angle SMO = \\angle SNO = 90^\\circ \\). Consequently, \\( K, L, M, N \\) all lie on the same spherical plane intersecting every face, concluding on a common circular perimeter being spherical-cap.\n\n10. **Final Conclusion:**\n    - Therefore, \\( K, L, M, N \\) as orthogonal projections on tetrahedron faces lie jointly on a manified circumference concluding:\n\n\\(\\boxed{K, L, M, \\text{and} N \\text{ lie on the same circle}}.\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots \\) be a sequence satisfying the condition that \\( a_{1} = 1 \\) and \\( a_{n} = 10 a_{n-1} - 1 \\) for all \\( n \\geq 2 \\). Find the minimum \\( n \\) such that \\( a_{n} > 10^{100} \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem step by step. The problem is about a sequence where the first term is 1, and each subsequent term is 10 times the previous term minus 1. We need to find the smallest n such that the nth term exceeds 10^100. Hmm, that seems pretty big. Let me write down the given information to get started.\n\nSo, the sequence is defined recursively:\n- a\u2081 = 1\n- a\u2099 = 10a\u2099\u208b\u2081 - 1 for n \u2265 2\n\nOur goal is to find the minimal n where a\u2099 > 10^100.\n\nFirst, maybe I should try to find a closed-form formula for a\u2099. Recursive relations can sometimes be solved by finding their homogeneous and particular solutions. Let me recall how linear recursions work. The general form for a linear recurrence is a\u2099 + c\u2081a\u2099\u208b\u2081 + ... + c\u2096a\u2099\u208b\u2096 = f(n). In our case, the recurrence is a\u2099 = 10a\u2099\u208b\u2081 - 1, which can be rewritten as a\u2099 - 10a\u2099\u208b\u2081 = -1. So it's a linear nonhomogeneous recurrence relation of order 1.\n\nThe standard method to solve such a recurrence is to find the solution to the homogeneous equation and then find a particular solution. The homogeneous equation here is a\u2099 - 10a\u2099\u208b\u2081 = 0. The characteristic equation would be r - 10 = 0, so r = 10. Therefore, the general solution to the homogeneous equation is A*10\u207f, where A is a constant.\n\nNow, for the particular solution, since the nonhomogeneous term is a constant (-1), we can try a constant particular solution. Let's assume the particular solution is a constant C. Plugging into the recurrence:\n\nC - 10C = -1 \u21d2 -9C = -1 \u21d2 C = 1/9.\n\nSo, the general solution to the recurrence is the sum of the homogeneous and particular solutions:\n\na\u2099 = A*10\u207f + 1/9.\n\nNow, apply the initial condition to find A. When n = 1, a\u2081 = 1:\n\n1 = A*10\u00b9 + 1/9 \u21d2 1 = 10A + 1/9 \u21d2 10A = 1 - 1/9 = 8/9 \u21d2 A = (8/9)/10 = 8/90 = 4/45.\n\nTherefore, the closed-form formula is:\n\na\u2099 = (4/45)*10\u207f + 1/9.\n\nLet me check this formula with the first few terms to ensure it's correct.\n\nFor n = 1: (4/45)*10 + 1/9 = 40/45 + 5/45 = 45/45 = 1. Correct.\n\nFor n = 2: (4/45)*100 + 1/9 = 400/45 + 5/45 = 405/45 = 9. But according to the recursive formula, a\u2082 = 10a\u2081 - 1 = 10*1 -1 = 9. Correct.\n\nFor n = 3: (4/45)*1000 + 1/9 = 4000/45 + 5/45 = 4005/45 = 89. According to recursion: a\u2083 = 10a\u2082 -1 = 10*9 -1 = 89. Correct. Good, the formula seems right.\n\nSo, now we have a\u2099 = (4/45)10\u207f + 1/9. We need to find the minimal n such that a\u2099 > 10^100.\n\nLet's set up the inequality:\n\n(4/45)10\u207f + 1/9 > 10^100.\n\nWe can solve this inequality for n. Let's first rearrange terms:\n\n(4/45)10\u207f > 10^100 - 1/9.\n\nBut 10^100 is an extremely large number, and 1/9 is negligible compared to it. However, since we need the exact minimal n, maybe we need to be careful here. Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can approximate 10^100 - 1/9 \u2248 10^100. But let's verify if that approximation is valid.\n\nSuppose that (4/45)10\u207f > 10^100 - 1/9.\n\nThen, 10\u207f > (45/4)(10^100 - 1/9).\n\nBut 10^100 is 1 followed by 100 zeros, so subtracting 1/9 would be like 10^100 minus approximately 0.111..., which is negligible. So, (45/4)(10^100 - 1/9) \u2248 (45/4)10^100.\n\nBut let's not approximate yet. Let's see if we can solve the inequality exactly.\n\nStarting with:\n\n(4/45)10\u207f + 1/9 > 10^100.\n\nMultiply both sides by 45 to eliminate denominators:\n\n4*10\u207f + 5 > 45*10^100.\n\nSo,\n\n4*10\u207f > 45*10^100 - 5.\n\nAgain, 45*10^100 is 4.5*10^101, and subtracting 5 is negligible. So, 4*10\u207f > 45*10^100 - 5 \u2248 45*10^100.\n\nTherefore, 10\u207f > (45/4)10^100.\n\nBut let's do this more precisely. Let's write 45*10^100 - 5 as 45*10^100 - 5. Then, 4*10\u207f > 45*10^100 - 5.\n\nTo solve for n, we can 