d \\text{or} \\quad k \\geq 4\n     \\]\n     Therefore, the values of $k$ are:\n     \\[\n     k \\in (-\\infty, 1] \\cup \\{4\\} \\cup [5, +\\infty)\n     \\]\n     Here, we note that although $k = 4$ satisfies $x \\geq 2\\pi$, $k=4$ is redundant since $5$ already falls in the valid interval.\n     Thus:\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{\\pi k}{2}, \\quad k \\in (-\\infty, 1] \\cup [5, +\\infty)\n     \\]\n\n2. We further analyze $\\sin 2x = \\frac{1}{2}$ with $x \\in (\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi]$.\n   \n   Substituting $\\theta = 2x$, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 2x = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   This condition implies:\n   \\[\n   2x = \\frac{\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi k \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 2x = \\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi k = \\frac{5\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi k\n   \\]\n   Thus,\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\pi k \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\pi k\n   \\]\n\n   Given $x \\in (\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi]$, let's examine valid $k$ values:\n   - For $x = \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\pi k$, $\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\pi k \\leq \\pi \\implies k \\in (\\frac{5}{12},1]$, no valid integer $k$.\n   - For $x = \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\pi k$, $\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\pi k \\leq \\pi \\implies k \\in (\\frac{1}{12}, \\frac{7}{12}]$, no valid integer $k$.\n\n   No solution exists in this case.\n\n3. Analyzing $\\sin 2x = -1$ within $x \\in (\\pi, 2\\pi]$:\n   \n   For $\\sin \\theta = -1$, we know:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\frac{3\\pi}{2} + 2\\pi k\n   \\]\n   Substituting $\\theta = 2x$, we get:\n   \\[\n   2x = \\frac{3\\pi}{2} + 2\\pi k\n   \\]\n   Solving for $x$:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{3\\pi}{4} + \\pi k\n   \\]\n   For $x \\in (\\pi, 2\\pi]$, after substituting multiple values of $k$, we find that:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{7\\pi}{4} \\quad \\text{for} \\quad k = 1\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{x = \\frac{\\pi k}{2}, \\, k \\in (-\\infty, 1] \\cup [5, +\\infty)} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\boxed{x = \\frac{7\\pi}{4}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source