onstraints. Let's formalize this.\n\nFor any two distinct residues a and b \u2208 {1,2,3,4}, if color(a) \u2260 color(b), then color(|a - b| mod5) must be red.\n\nNote that |a - b| mod5 is equivalent to (a - b) mod5 if a > b, else (b - a) mod5. But since we're taking absolute value, it's the same as |a - b| mod5.\n\nThis adds more implications to our existing constraints. Specifically, for each pair of residues a and b (a \u2260 b):\n\nIf color(a) \u2260 color(b), then color(|a - b| mod5) = R.\n\nPreviously, from rule b), we had that if color(a) \u2260 color(b), then color(a*b mod5) = B.\n\nNow, we also have that if color(a) \u2260 color(b), then color(|a - b| mod5) = R.\n\nTherefore, combining both rules a) and b), whenever two residues a and b are colored differently, we must have:\n\n- The residue c = a*b mod5 is blue.\n\n- The residue d = |a - b| mod5 is red.\n\nTherefore, for any a \u2260 b, if color(a) \u2260 color(b), then color(c)=B and color(d)=R.\n\nThis significantly increases the number of constraints.\n\nOur previous analysis only considered the product rule (rule b)), but we neglected the subtraction rule (rule a)). Therefore, we need to redo the analysis with both rules.\n\nThis makes the problem more complex. Let's try to tackle it.\n\nFirst, let's list all pairs of residues a and b (a < b) and compute both c = a*b mod5 and d = |a - b| mod5.\n\nPairs (a,b):\n\n1. (1,2): c=2, d=1\n2. (1,3): c=3, d=2\n3. (1,4): c=4, d=3\n4. (2,3): c=1, d=1\n5. (2,4): c=3, d=2\n6. (3,4): c=2, d=1\n\nNow, for each pair, if they are colored differently, then c must be blue and d must be red.\n\nLet's see what this implies.\n\nTake pair (1,2):\n\nIf color(1) \u2260 color(2), then:\n\n- c=2 must be blue (from rule b))\n\n- d=1 must be red (from rule a))\n\nSimilarly, pair (1,3):\n\nIf color(1) \u2260 color(3):\n\n- c=3 must be blue\n\n- d=2 must be red\n\nPair (1,4):\n\nIf color(1) \u2260 color(4):\n\n- c=4 must be blue\n\n- d=3 must be red\n\nPair (2,3):\n\nIf color(2) \u2260 color(3):\n\n- c=1 must be blue\n\n- d=1 must be red\n\nPair (2,4):\n\nIf color(2) \u2260 color(4):\n\n- c=3 must be blue\n\n- d=2 must be red\n\nPair (3,4):\n\nIf color(3) \u2260 color(4):\n\n- c=2 must be blue\n\n- d=1 must be red\n\nNow, we need to ensure that for any pair of residues a and b, if they are colored differently, the corresponding c and d residues have the required colors.\n\nThis adds multiple new constraints. Let's try to model this.\n\nWe can consider each pair and their implications. Let's start by considering the possible colorings and checking these constraints.\n\nBut this might be too time-consuming. Instead, perhaps we can find a consistent coloring by ensuring that all these implications are satisfied.\n\nLet's consider the previous colorings we thought were valid and check them with these new constraints.\n\nFirst, all residues blue (B,B,B,B):\n\n- No pairs are different colors, so no implications apply. Valid.\n\nAll residues red (R,R,R,R):\n\n- Similarly, no pairs are different colors. Valid.\n\nNow, consider the coloring (R,B,B,R):\n\nResidues 1=R, 2=B, 3=B, 4=R.\n\nCheck all pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): different. Need c=2=B, d=1=R. Here, c=2 is B (ok), d=1 is R (ok). Good.\n\n2. (1,3): different. Need c=3=B, d=2=B. Here, c=3=B (ok), but d=2=B, which should be R. Conflict. Therefore, invalid.\n\nThus, this coloring is invalid because pair (1,3) are different colors, requiring d=2 to be red, but residue2 is blue.\n\nSimilarly, check the other colorings:\n\nColoring (R,B,B,B):\n\nResidues 1=R, 2=B, 3=B, 4=B.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): different. c=2=B (ok), d=1=R (ok). Good.\n\n2. (1,3): different. c=3=B (ok), d=2=B (needs to be R). Conflict. Invalid.\n\n3. (1,4): different. c=4=B (ok), d=3=B (needs to be R). Conflict. Invalid.\n\n4. (2,3): same. No problem.\n\n5. (2,4): same. No problem.\n\n6. (3,4): same. No problem.\n\nSo this coloring is invalid due to pairs (1,3) and (1,4).\n\nTherefore, the only valid colorings are all blue and all red?\n\nWait, but all red:\n\nCheck pair (1,2): same color. Ok.\n\nBut wait, no: in all red, all residues are R. So no pairs are different, so no implications. Valid.\n\nSimilarly, all blue: valid.\n\nBut what about other colorings? Are there any colorings where some residues are different but still satisfy all constraints?\n\nLet\u2019s try to find such a coloring.\n\nSuppose we try to set residue1=R, and other residues=B. Let's see:\n\nResidues: 1=R,2=B,3=B,4=B.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): different. Need c=2=B, d=1=R. Ok.\n\n2. (1,3): different. Need c=3=B, d=2=R. But d=2 is B. Conflict.\n\nSo invalid.\n\nAnother try: residue1=R, residue2=R, residue3=B, residue4=B.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): same. Ok.\n\n2. (1,3): different. Need c=3=B, d=2=R. But d=2 is R (ok), c=3=B (ok). Ok.\n\n3. (1,4): different. Need c=4=B, d=3=B. But d=3 must be R. Conflict.\n\nSo invalid.\n\nAnother try: residue1=R, residue2=B, residue3=R, residue4=B.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): different. Need c=2=B, d=1=R. Ok.\n\n2. (1,3): different. Need c=3=R (must be B). Conflict.\n\n3. (1,4): different. Need c=4=B, d=3=R. Ok.\n\n4. (2,3): different. Need c=1=B, d=1=R. So c=1 must be B, but residue1=R. Conflict.\n\n5. (2,4): different. Need c=3=B (residue3=R). Conflict.\n\n6. (3,4): different. Need c=2=B (residue2=B), d=1=R. Ok.\n\nSo multiple conflicts. Invalid.\n\nAnother try: residue1=B, residue2=R, residue3=R, residue4=R.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): different. Need c=2=R (must be B). Conflict.\n\nSo invalid.\n\nPerhaps try a coloring where two residues are R and two are B, carefully chosen.\n\nSuppose residues1 and4 are R, residues2 and3 are B.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): different. Need c=2=B, d=1=R. Ok.\n\n2. (1,3): different. Need c=3=B, d=2=B. d=2 must be R. Conflict.\n\nSo invalid.\n\nAnother option: residues1 and3 are R, residues2 and4 are B.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): different. c=2=B, d=1=R. Ok.\n\n2. (1,3): same. Ok.\n\n3. (1,4): different. c=4=B, d=3=R. residue3=R (ok), c=4=B (ok). Ok.\n\n4. (2,3): different. c=1=B, d=1=R. residue1=R (ok), c=1 must be B. But residue1=R. Conflict.\n\n5. (2,4): same. Ok.\n\n6. (3,4): different. c=2=B, d=1=R. Ok.\n\nSo conflict in pair (2,3): need c=1=B but residue1=R. Invalid.\n\nAnother attempt: residues1 and2 are R, residues3 and4 are B.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): same. Ok.\n\n2. (1,3): different. c=3=B, d=2=R. Ok.\n\n3. (1,4): different. c=4=B, d=3=R. Ok.\n\n4. (2,3): different. c=1=B, d=1=R. residue1=R (must be B). Conflict.\n\n5. (2,4): different. c=3=B, d=2=R. Ok.\n\n6. (3,4): same. Ok.\n\nConflict in pair (2,3).\n\nThis seems very restrictive. Let's see if there's any coloring apart from all R or all B that satisfies all constraints.\n\nSuppose we set residue1=R, residue2=B, residue3=R, residue4=B.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): different. c=2=B, d=1=R. Ok.\n\n2. (1,3): different. c=3=R (must be B). Conflict.\n\n3. (1,4): different. c=4=B, d=3=R. Ok.\n\n4. (2,3): different. c=1=B, d=1=R. Conflict.\n\n5. (2,4): different. c=3=R (must be B). Conflict.\n\n6. (3,4): different. c=2=B, d=1=R. Ok.\n\nMultiple conflicts. Invalid.\n\nAnother try: residues1=R, residue2=B, residue3=B, residue4=R.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): different. c=2=B, d=1=R. Ok.\n\n2. (1,3): different. c=3=B, d=2=B (must be R). Conflict.\n\n3. (1,4): same. Ok.\n\n4. (2,3): same. Ok.\n\n5. (2,4): different. c=3=B, d=2=B (must be R). Conflict.\n\n6. (3,4): different. c=2=B, d=1=R. Ok.\n\nConflicts in pairs (1,3) and (2,4).\n\nThis seems impossible. Is there any coloring other than all R or all B that satisfies all constraints?\n\nLet\u2019s try residue1=B, residue2=R, residue3=R, residue4=B.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): different. c=2=R (must be B). Conflict.\n\nInvalid.\n\nAlternatively, residues1=B, residue2=R, residue3=B, residue4=R.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n1. (1,2): different. c=2=R (must be B). Conflict.\n\nInvalid.\n\nHmm. It seems that any coloring where two residues are different leads to a conflict. Let's try to prove that only all-R or all-B are valid.\n\nAssume that all residues are the same color. Then, no pairs are different, so all constraints are satisfied. Valid.\n\nNow, suppose there exists at least one pair of residues with different colors. Let's assume that residue1 is R and residue2 is B. Then:\n\nFrom pair (1,2):\n\n- c=2 must be B (ok).\n\n- d=1 must be R (ok).\n\nBut then consider pair (1,3). If residue3 is B:\n\n- Then, from pair (1,3):\n\n- c=3 must be B (ok).\n\n- d=2 must be R. But residue2 is B. Conflict.\n\nIf residue3 is R:\n\n- Then, consider pair (2,3):\n\n- If residue3=R and residue2=B:\n\n- c=1 must be B. But residue1=R. Conflict.\n\nTherefore, if residue1=R and residue2=B, residue3 must be B (causing conflict in d=2), or R (causing conflict in c=1).\n\nTherefore, no valid coloring with residue1=R and residue2=B.\n\nSimilarly, if we assume any two residues are different, we end up in contradictions.\n\nTherefore, the only valid colorings are all-R and all-B.\n\nBut wait, all-B is given for multiples of 5, but the problem states that chip5 is blue, which is part of the multiples of 5. If we choose all-B, then all residues are blue, which includes the multiples of 5. But the problem allows coloring the remaining chips (non-multiples of 5) with red or blue, but under the constraints. But in our analysis, the only valid colorings are all-B or all-R for the non-multiples of 5.\n\nWait, but the multiples of 5 are already forced to be blue. So, if we choose all-B for the residues, then all chips are blue. If we choose all-R for the residues, then non-multiples are red and multiples are blue.\n\nBut do these satisfy all constraints?\n\nLet's check all-R for residues (non-multiples are red, multiples are blue).\n\nTake any two chips. If they are both red or both blue, no problem. If one is red and the other is blue, then:\n\nFor rule a): |x - y| must be red.\n\nIf x is red (non-multiple of 5) and y is blue (multiple of 5):\n\n|x - y| = a non-multiple of 5 (since multiple - non-multiple is non-multiple). Therefore, |x - y| is red, as required.\n\nFor rule b): x*y must be blue. Since x is non-multiple and y is multiple, x*y is multiple, which is blue. Okay.\n\nTherefore, all-R coloring for residues is valid.\n\nSimilarly, all-B coloring for residues would mean all chips are blue. This satisfies all constraints trivially, as there are no differing colors.\n\nBut wait, the problem states that chip5 is blue. If all residues are blue, then all chips are blue. This is allowed, but the problem says \"the remaining chips\" can be colored. So initially, chip5 is blue, and the rest can be colored following the rules. But through the rules, we derived that all multiples of 5 must be blue, and then the residues must all be the same color. So, the only valid colorings are all non-multiples red and multiples blue, or all chips blue.\n\nBut wait, if all residues are blue, then all chips are blue, including the multiples of 5. This is allowed since the problem only requires that chip5 is blue; the others can be blue or red, but in this case, they're forced to be blue.\n\nBut in the all-R residues case, non-multiples are red and multiples are blue. This also satisfies the rules.\n\nTherefore, there are two valid colorings: all blue, or non-multiples red and multiples blue.\n\nBut wait, this contradicts our earlier analysis where we thought there were four colorings, but after considering rule a), we realized there are only two.\n\nYes, correct. The correct number is two.\n\nBut wait, the original analysis with four colorings didn't consider rule a) properly. After considering rule a), we found that only all-R and all-B are valid.\n\nBut let's verify this carefully.\n\nIf all residues are blue (B,B,B,B), then all chips are blue. This satisfies all constraints, as there are no differing colors.\n\nIf residues are all red (R,R,R,R), then non-multiples are red and multiples are blue. Let's check rule a) and b):\n\nTake two chips of different colors: one red (non-multiple) and one blue (multiple). Then:\n\n- Rule a): |x - y| must be red. Since x is non-multiple and y is multiple, |x - y| is non-multiple, hence red. Okay.\n\n- Rule b): x*y must be blue. x is non-multiple, y is multiple, so x*y is multiple, hence blue. Okay.\n\nTherefore, this coloring is valid.\n\nAny other coloring where some residues are different leads to contradictions in either rule a) or b).\n\nTherefore, there are only two valid colorings: all blue, or non-multiples red and multiples blue.\n\nBut wait, the problem states that chip5 is blue, but doesn't say anything about the other multiples of 5. However, through rule a), we derived that all multiples of 5 must be blue. Therefore, in any valid coloring, multiples of 5 are blue. The only freedom is in the non-multiples. If we color all non-multiples red, that's valid. If we color all non-multiples blue, that's also valid (but they are already blue due to being multiples of 5 or not).\n\nWait, but if all non-multiples are blue, then all chips are blue, which includes the multiples of 5. That's allowed. So the two colorings are:\n\n1. All chips blue.\n\n2. Multiples of 5 blue, non-multiples red.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 2.\n\nBut let's confirm once more with an example.\n\nSuppose all non-multiples are red:\n\nTake x=1 (red) and y=6 (red). Same color, no issue.\n\nTake x=1 (red) and y=5 (blue). Different colors. |1-5|=4 (red), which is correct. x*y=5 (blue), which is correct.\n\nTake x=2 (red) and y=7 (red). Same color.\n\nTake x=2 (red) and y=10 (blue). Different colors. |2-10|=8 (red), correct. x*y=20 (blue), correct.\n\nTake x=6 (red) and y=10 (blue). Different colors. |6-10|=4 (red), correct. x*y=60, which is >50, so rule b) doesn't apply.\n\nAll constraints are satisfied.\n\nIf all chips are blue:\n\nAny two chips are blue. No constraints violated.\n\nTherefore, the two colorings are valid.\n\nThus, the number of valid colorings is 2.\n\nBut wait, earlier we thought all residues must be the same color, leading to two possibilities. However, initially, we considered four possibilities but realized we missed constraints. After correcting, we arrived at two valid colorings.\n\nBut the original problem states that chip5 is blue. If we color all chips blue, this satisfies the condition. If we color non-multiples red and multiples blue, this also satisfies the condition. Therefore, the answer is 2.\n\nBut wait, the problem says \"the remaining chips\", i.e., chips other than chip5, need to be colored following the rules. But we derived that all multiples of 5 must be blue due to rule a). So chip10, chip15, etc., are forced to be blue. Therefore, in the coloring where all non-multiples are red, the multiples of 5 (including chip5) are blue. In the coloring where all non-multiples are blue, the multiples of 5 are also blue. So both colorings are possible.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 2.\n\nBut to confirm, let's check if there are any other colorings. Suppose we try to color some residues red and others blue, but in a way that satisfies all constraints.\n\nBut based on the previous analysis, it's impossible. Any differing colors in residues lead to contradictions in either rule a) or b). Therefore, only the all-R and all-B colorings for the residues are possible.\n\nTherefore, the total number of valid colorings is 2.", "deepseek_solution": "The problem involves coloring chips numbered 1 to 50 with two colors, red or blue, under specific rules. Chip 5 is given as blue. The key steps to determine the valid colorings are:\n\n1. **Multiples of 5 Must Be Blue**: Rule a) enforces that if two chips differ by 5, they must be the same color. Starting from chip 5 (blue), all multiples of 5 (10, 15, ..., 50) are forced to be blue to avoid contradictions.\n\n2. **Residue Classes Modulo 5**: Non-multiples of 5 form residue classes modulo 5 (1, 2, 3, 4). Each residue class must be uniformly colored due to rule a), as pairs differing by 5 must share the same color.\n\n3. **Constraints from Rules a) and b)**:\n   - **Rule a)**: If two chips differ in color, their difference's residue class must be red.\n   - **Rule b)**: If two chips differ in color and their product is \u226450, the product's residue class must be blue.\n\n4. **Analyzing Residue Interactions**:\n   - For residues 1, 2, 3, 4, any differing colors lead to contradictions. For example, if residue 1 is red and residue 2 is blue, their product (residue 2) must be blue (satisfied), but their difference (residue 1) must be red (satisfied). However, other residue pairs (e.g., residue 1 and 3) introduce conflicts in either the product or difference residues, making such colorings invalid.\n\n5. **Valid Colorings**:\n   - **All Blue**: All chips are blue, satisfying all rules trivially.\n   - **Non-Multiples Red, Multiples Blue**: Non-multiples of 5 are red, and multiples are blue. This satisfies both rules, as differences and products involving mixed colors comply with the required colors.\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nThere are **2** valid colorings possible.  \n$\\boxed{2}$", "ground_truth_solution": "1. We start with 50 chips numbered from 1 to 50. According to the given rules, we need to color each chip either red or blue. We know that chip 5 is blue.\n2. Let's analyze the rules for coloring:\n    a) If the chips numbered \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are of different colors, then the chip numbered \\(|x-y|\\) is painted red.\n    b) If the chips numbered \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are of different colors and \\(x \\cdot y\\) is a number between 1 and 50 (inclusive), then the chip numbered \\(x \\cdot y\\) is painted blue.\n\n3. Next, observe that any two numbers differing by 5 will have the same color. \n   - If two numbers \\(x\\) and \\(x+5\\) were of different colors, then according to rule (a), the chip numbered \\(|x - (x+5)| = 5\\) would need to be red. But we know chip 5 is blue, which is a contradiction.\n   - Therefore, numbers differing by 5 must be the same color.\n\n4. Given that we only need to determine the colors of the first four chips (1, 2, 3, 4) because all other chips will follow by the rule that numbers differing by 5 have the same color.\n\n5. We consider two cases based on the color of chip 1:\n    - **Case 1**: Chip 1 is blue.\n        - If chip 1 is blue, then using rule (b), we can see that all other numbers must also be blue.\n        - If any chip \\(k \\neq 1\\) were red, then \\(k \\cdot 1 = k\\) should be blue (rule (b)), leading to a contradiction since \\(k\\) was assumed red.\n        - Thus, all chips must be blue.\n\n    - **Case 2**: Chip 1 is red.\n        - By rule (a), the chip numbered \\(5-1=4\\) must be red (as chips 1 and 5 are different colors).\n        - To determine the color of chips 2 and 3, we assume chip 3 is blue:\n            - Then \\(2 = 3 - 1\\) must be red, as 3 and 1 are different colors.\n            - But \\(3\\) and \\(5-2=3\\) should have different colors, as this leads to 3 being red (contradicting our assumption that 3 is blue). Thus, chip 3 cannot be blue.\n        - Therefore, chip 3 is red, and by analogous reasoning, so is chip 2.\n\n6. Conclusion:\n    - When chip 1 is blue, all chips are blue: \\(\\{1, 2, \\ldots, 50\\}\\).\n    - When chip 1 is red, chips \\(\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13, 14, \\ldots \\}\\) are all red and multiples of 5 are blue.\n\n7. Therefore, there are only two distinct colorations possible:\n    1. All chips are blue.\n    2. All chips are red, except the multiples of 5 which are blue.\n\n\\(\\boxed{2}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the coordinates of point \\( M(x ; y ; z) \\), which is symmetric to point \\( B_{1}(5 ; 8 ;-3) \\) with respect to the line defined by points \\( B(3 ; 4 ; 1) \\) and \\( A_{1}(1 ; 6 ;-7) \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the coordinates of point M, which is the symmetric point of B\u2081(5, 8, -3) with respect to the line passing through points B(3, 4, 1) and A\u2081(1, 6, -7). Hmm, symmetry with respect to a line in 3D space. Let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, I recall that a symmetric point with respect to a line means that the line is the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the original point and its symmetric counterpart. So, point M should be such that the line BM (if B is on the line) is perpendicular to the given line, and the midpoint of B\u2081M lies on the given line. Wait, actually, the line in question here is defined by points B and A\u2081, right? So, the line BA\u2081 is the axis of symmetry. Therefore, point M is such that the line BA\u2081 is the perpendicular bisector of the segment B\u2081M. That means two things: the midpoint of B\u2081M lies on line BA\u2081, and the vector B\u2081M is perpendicular to the direction vector of line BA\u2081.\n\nLet me verify that. If M is the reflection of B\u2081 over line BA\u2081, then indeed, the line BA\u2081 should be the perpendicular bisector. Therefore, the midpoint between B\u2081 and M must lie on BA\u2081, and the line segment B\u2081M must be perpendicular to BA\u2081. So, I need to find M such that these two conditions are satisfied.\n\nFirst, let me find the parametric equations of line BA\u2081. Points B(3,4,1) and A\u2081(1,6,-7). The direction vector of BA\u2081 is A\u2081 - B, which is (1-3, 6-4, -7-1) = (-2, 2, -8). So, the direction vector is (-2, 2, -8). We can write the parametric equations of the line as:\n\nx = 3 - 2t\n\ny = 4 + 2t\n\nz = 1 - 8t\n\nwhere t is a parameter.\n\nAlternatively, we can write it in vector form as:\n\nr = B + t*(A\u2081 - B) = (3,4,1) + t*(-2,2,-8)\n\nNow, the midpoint of B\u2081M should lie on this line. Let M = (x, y, z). The midpoint is ((5 + x)/2, (8 + y)/2, (-3 + z)/2). This point must satisfy the parametric equations of line BA\u2081. Therefore:\n\n(5 + x)/2 = 3 - 2t\n\n(8 + y)/2 = 4 + 2t\n\n(-3 + z)/2 = 1 - 8t\n\nThese are three equations. Additionally, the vector B\u2081M must be perpendicular to the direction vector of BA\u2081. The vector B\u2081M is (x - 5, y - 8, z + 3). The direction vector of BA\u2081 is (-2, 2, -8). Their dot product should be zero:\n\n(x - 5)(-2) + (y - 8)(2) + (z + 3)(-8) = 0\n\nSo, we have four equations:\n\n1. (5 + x)/2 = 3 - 2t\n\n2. (8 + y)/2 = 4 + 2t\n\n3. (-3 + z)/2 = 1 - 8t\n\n4. -2(x - 5) + 2(y - 8) -8(z + 3) = 0\n\nLet me first solve the first three equations for x, y, z in terms of t.\n\nFrom equation 1:\n\n(5 + x)/2 = 3 - 2t\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n5 + x = 6 - 4t\n\nSo, x = 6 - 4t -5 = 1 - 4t\n\nFrom equation 2:\n\n(8 + y)/2 = 4 + 2t\n\nMultiply by 2:\n\n8 + y = 8 + 4t\n\nSo, y = 8 + 4t -8 = 4t\n\nFrom equation 3:\n\n(-3 + z)/2 = 1 - 8t\n\nMultiply by 2:\n\n-3 + z = 2 - 16t\n\nThus, z = 2 -16t +3 = 5 -16t\n\nSo, we have expressions for x, y, z in terms of t:\n\nx = 1 -4t\n\ny = 4t\n\nz =5 -16t\n\nNow, substitute these into equation 4:\n\n-2(x -5) +2(y -8) -8(z +3)=0\n\nLet's compute each term:\n\nx -5 = (1 -4t) -5 = -4 -4t\n\ny -8 = 4t -8\n\nz +3 = (5 -16t) +3 = 8 -16t\n\nSubstituting into equation 4:\n\n-2*(-4 -4t) +2*(4t -8) -8*(8 -16t) =0\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: -2*(-4 -4t) = 8 +8t\n\nSecond term: 2*(4t -8) =8t -16\n\nThird term: -8*(8 -16t)= -64 +128t\n\nNow, add all together:\n\n8 +8t +8t -16 -64 +128t =0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(8 -16 -64) + (8t +8t +128t) =0\n\n(-72) + (144t) =0\n\nSo:\n\n144t -72 =0\n\n144t =72\n\nt=72/144=1/2\n\nSo, t=1/2\n\nNow, substitute t=1/2 into expressions for x, y, z:\n\nx=1 -4*(1/2)=1 -2= -1\n\ny=4*(1/2)=2\n\nz=5 -16*(1/2)=5 -8= -3\n\nWait, so M is (-1, 2, -3)? But wait, let's check if this is correct. But point B\u2081 is (5,8,-3), so the midpoint between B\u2081 and M should be ((5 + (-1))/2, (8 +2)/2, (-3 + (-3))/2)=(4/2,10/2,-6/2)=(2,5,-3). Now, check if this midpoint (2,5,-3) lies on line BA\u2081.\n\nThe parametric equations of BA\u2081 are x=3-2t, y=4+2t, z=1-8t. Let's see if (2,5,-3) is on BA\u2081.\n\nSet x=3-2t=2 \u2192 3-2t=2 \u2192 -2t= -1 \u2192 t=1/2\n\nCheck y=4+2*(1/2)=4+1=5. Correct.\n\nCheck z=1 -8*(1/2)=1 -4= -3. Correct. So, the midpoint is indeed on BA\u2081. That's good.\n\nNow, check if vector B\u2081M is perpendicular to direction vector of BA\u2081.\n\nVector B\u2081M is M - B\u2081 = (-1 -5, 2 -8, -3 -(-3))= (-6, -6, 0)\n\nDirection vector of BA\u2081 is (-2,2,-8). Dot product:\n\n(-6)(-2) + (-6)(2) +0*(-8)=12 -12 +0=0. Yes, they are perpendicular. Perfect.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of point M are (-1, 2, -3). But let me verify again.\n\nWait, original point B\u2081 is (5,8,-3). The symmetric point M is (-1,2,-3). The midpoint is (2,5,-3). Then, from line BA\u2081, at t=1/2, we reach point (3 -2*(1/2),4 +2*(1/2),1 -8*(1/2))=(3-1,4+1,1-4)=(2,5,-3), which is the midpoint. So everything checks out.\n\nBut wait, wait a second. The z-coordinate of M is -3, same as B\u2081. Is that possible?\n\nWell, since the line BA\u2081 might not be horizontal, but depending on the direction. Let me see. The direction vector is (-2,2,-8), so it does have a z-component. However, in this case, since the midpoint's z-coordinate is -3, and the original point B\u2081 is at z=-3, so the reflection would also have z=-3. Because the line BA\u2081 passes through the midpoint (2,5,-3). So if moving along BA\u2081, when t=1/2, we reach (2,5,-3). So yes, since the midpoint is at z=-3, and B\u2081 is at z=-3, then M must also be at z=-3. So that's okay.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the reflection in 3D over a line can result in same z-coordinate if the line's direction allows for that. So I think the result is correct.\n\nTherefore, after all the calculations, the coordinates of M are (-1, 2, -3).\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe coordinates of point \\( M \\) are \\(\\boxed{(-1 ; 2 ; -3)}\\).", "deepseek_solution": "To find the coordinates of point \\( M(x ; y ; z) \\), which is symmetric to point \\( B_{1}(5 ; 8 ;-3) \\) with respect to the line defined by points \\( B(3 ; 4 ; 1) \\) and \\( A_{1}(1 ; 6 ;-7) \\), we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the direction vector of the line BA\u2081**:\n   The direction vector is \\( A_{1} - B = (1-3, 6-4, -7-1) = (-2, 2, -8) \\).\n\n2. **Write the parametric equations of the line BA\u2081**:\n   \\[\n   x = 3 - 2t, \\quad y = 4 + 2t, \\quad z = 1 - 8t\n   \\]\n\n3. **Express the midpoint of \\( B_{1} \\) and \\( M \\) in terms of \\( t \\)**:\n   The midpoint \\( \\left( \\frac{5+x}{2}, \\frac{8+y}{2}, \\frac{-3+z}{2} \\right) \\) must lie on the line BA\u2081. This gives:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5+x}{2} = 3 - 2t, \\quad \\frac{8+y}{2} = 4 + 2t, \\quad \\frac{-3+z}{2} = 1 - 8t\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\( x, y, z \\) in terms of \\( t \\)**:\n   \\[\n   x = 1 - 4t, \\quad y = 4t, \\quad z = 5 - 16t\n   \\]\n\n5. **Ensure the vector \\( B_{1}M \\) is perpendicular to the direction vector of BA\u2081**:\n   The vector \\( B_{1}M = (x-5, y-8, z+3) \\) must have a dot product of zero with \\( (-2, 2, -8) \\):\n   \\[\n   -2(x - 5) + 2(y - 8) - 8(z + 3) = 0\n   \\]\n\n6. **Substitute \\( x, y, z \\) into the perpendicularity condition and solve for \\( t \\)**:\n   Substituting \\( x = 1 - 4t \\), \\( y = 4t \\), \\( z = 5 - 16t \\) into the equation:\n   \\[\n   -2(-4 - 4t) + 2(4t - 8) - 8(8 - 16t) = 0\n   \\]\n   Simplifying gives \\( 144t - 72 = 0 \\), leading to \\( t = \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\n7. **Find \\( x, y, z \\) using \\( t = \\frac{1}{2} \\)**:\n   \\[\n   x = 1 - 4 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = -1, \\quad y = 4 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 2, \\quad z = 5 - 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = -3\n   \\]\n\n8. **Verify the midpoint and perpendicularity**:\n   The midpoint \\( (2, 5, -3) \\) lies on line BA\u2081, and the vector \\( B_{1}M \\) is perpendicular to the direction vector of BA\u2081.\n\nThus, the coordinates of point \\( M \\) are \\(\\boxed{(-1 ; 2 ; -3)}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find the coordinates of point \\( M(x, y, z) \\), symmetric to point \\( B_1(5, 8, -3) \\) with respect to the line defined by points \\( B(3, 4, 1) \\) and \\( A_1(1, 6, -7) \\), we proceed as follows:\n\n1. Denote the line direction vector from \\( B \\) to \\( A_1 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\overline{B A_1} = A_1 - B = (1 - 3, 6 - 4, -7 - 1) = (-2, 2, -8)\n   \\]\n\n2. Parametrize point \\( H \\), the midpoint on the line \\( BA_1 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\overline{BH} = k \\overline{B A_1} = k(-2, 2, -8)\n   \\]\n   Force \\( \\overline{BH} = \\frac{1}{2} (\\overline{BB_1} + \\overline{BM}) \\), therefore we have:\n   \\[\n   k(-2, 2, -8) = \\frac{1}{2} (BB_1 + BM)\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\( \\overline{BB_1} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\overline{BB_1} = B_1 - B = (5 - 3, 8 - 4, -3 - 1) = (2, 4, -4)\n   \\]\n\n4. Write the equality from step 2:\n   \\[\n   k(-2, 2, -8) = \\frac{1}{2}(2, 4, -4) + \\frac{1}{2}(x - 3, y - 4, z - 1)\n   \\]\n\n5. This yields three equations:\n   \\[\n   -2k = 1 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - 3)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2k = 2 + \\frac{1}{2}(y - 4)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -8k = -2 + \\frac{1}{2}(z - 1)\n   \\]\n\n6. Simplify these equations:\n   For \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   -2k = 1 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - 3) \\implies -4k = 2 + x - 3 \\implies -4k = x - 1 \\implies x = -4k + 1\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(y\\):\n   \\[\n   2k = 2 + \\frac{1}{2}(y - 4) \\implies 4k = 4 + y - 4 \\implies 4k = y \\implies y = 4k + 4\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(z\\):\n   \\[\n   -8k = -2 + \\frac{1}{2}(z - 1) \\implies -16k = -4 + z - 1 \\implies -16k = z - 3 \\implies z = -16k + 3\n   \\]\n\n7. Use the symmetric condition that vector \\( \\overline{B_1M} \\) is orthogonal to vector \\( \\overline{BA_1} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\overline{B_1 M} \\cdot \\overline{B A_1} = 0\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   (x-5, y-8, z+3) \\cdot (-2, 2, -8) = 0\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   -2(x-5) + 2(y-8) - 8(z+3) = 0\n   \\]\n\n8. Substitute \\( x, y, z \\) from above into this condition:\n   \\[\n   -2(-4k + 1 - 5) + 2(4k + 4 - 8) - 8(-16k + 3 + 3) = 0\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   -2(-4k - 4) + 2(4k - 4) - 8(-16k + 6) = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   8k + 8 + 8k - 8 + 128k - 48 = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   144k - 48 = 0 \\implies 144k = 48 \\implies k = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n\n9. Substitute \\( k = \\frac{1}{3} \\) back into the coordinates \\( x, y, z \\):\n   \\[\n   x = -4\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) + 1 = -\\frac{4}{3} + 1 = -\\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = 4\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) + 4 = \\frac{4}{3} + 4 = \\frac{16}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z = -16\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) + 3 = -\\frac{16}{3} + 3 = -\\frac{7}{3}\n   \\]\n\nHence, the coordinates of point \\( M \\) are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{M\\left(-1, 2, -3\\right)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( n \\) be an integer between 1 and 1990 such that \\( x^2 + x - 3n \\) can be factored into the product of two linear factors with integer coefficients. How many such integers \\( n \\) are there?\n\n(A) 1990  \n(B) 75  \n(C) 50  \n(D) 44  \n(The 2nd \"Five Goat Cup\" Junior High School Mathematics Competition, 1990)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to solve this problem. So, we need to find the number of integers n between 1 and 1990 such that the quadratic equation x\u00b2 + x - 3n can be factored into the product of two linear factors with integer coefficients. The answer choices are given, but I need to figure out the correct one step by step.\n\nFirst, let me recall that if a quadratic polynomial ax\u00b2 + bx + c can be factored into two linear factors with integer coefficients, then it must be expressible as (mx + p)(nx + q), where m, n, p, q are integers. Expanding that, we get mnx\u00b2 + (mq + pn)x + pq. Comparing coefficients with ax\u00b2 + bx + c, we have a = mn, b = mq + pn, and c = pq.\n\nIn our case, the quadratic is x\u00b2 + x - 3n. So, a = 1, b = 1, and c = -3n. Since the leading coefficient is 1, the factors must be of the form (x + p)(x + q), where p and q are integers. Let me check that: (x + p)(x + q) = x\u00b2 + (p + q)x + pq. Comparing this with x\u00b2 + x - 3n, we have:\n\n1. p + q = 1 (coefficient of x)\n2. pq = -3n (constant term)\n\nSo, we need to find integers p and q such that their sum is 1 and their product is -3n. Then, for each such pair (p, q), n must be equal to -pq/3. Since n has to be an integer between 1 and 1990, this implies that -pq must be divisible by 3, and the value -pq/3 must be in the range 1 \u2264 -pq/3 \u2264 1990.\n\nSo, the key here is to find all integer pairs (p, q) such that p + q = 1 and pq is divisible by 3, then compute n = -pq/3 and count how many distinct n satisfy 1 \u2264 n \u2264 1990.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can express this in terms of variables. Let's let p be an integer, then q = 1 - p. Then pq = p(1 - p) = p - p\u00b2. So, pq = -p\u00b2 + p. Therefore, -pq = p\u00b2 - p. Then, n = (p\u00b2 - p)/3. So, n must be equal to (p\u00b2 - p)/3, and this must be an integer between 1 and 1990.\n\nSo, the problem reduces to finding all integers p such that (p\u00b2 - p) is divisible by 3, and (p\u00b2 - p)/3 is between 1 and 1990. Then, the number of such p (accounting for distinct n values) will give the answer.\n\nWait, but maybe different p's can give the same n. So, we need to be careful about counting distinct n's. Hmm. Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, since p and q are roots of the quadratic equation x\u00b2 + x - 3n = 0, then by Vieta's formula, p + q = -1 and pq = -3n? Wait, hold on, no. Wait, in the quadratic equation x\u00b2 + x - 3n = 0, the sum of the roots is -1 (since coefficient of x is 1, sum of roots is -b/a = -1/1 = -1) and the product is c/a = -3n/1 = -3n. So, in fact, if the roots are p and q, then p + q = -1 and pq = -3n. Wait, this contradicts my earlier conclusion where I thought p + q = 1. Wait, this is confusing.\n\nWait, let's start over. The quadratic equation is x\u00b2 + x - 3n. If it factors into (x + a)(x + b) where a and b are integers, then expanding gives x\u00b2 + (a + b)x + ab. Comparing coefficients:\n\n1. Coefficient of x: a + b = 1