ls gives us about 1.132, which is very close to the symbolic answer. Therefore, the symbolic answer is correct.\n\nTherefore, the definite integral is (25\u03c0)/12 - (25\u221a3)/8. We can leave it in that form or factor out 25/4 as mentioned before, but the problem might prefer the answer in the original separated terms.\n\nAlternatively, another approach to solve the integral could be integration by parts. Let me try that to cross-verify.\n\nLet me attempt integration by parts with u = x and dv = x / sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) dx.\n\nThen du = dx, and v = ?\n\nCompute v = \u222b x / sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) dx.\n\nLet me substitute w = 25 - x\u00b2, dw = -2x dx, so (-1/2) dw = x dx. Therefore, \u222b x / sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) dx = -1/2 \u222b dw / sqrt(w) = -1/2 * 2 sqrt(w) + C = -sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) + C.\n\nTherefore, v = -sqrt(25 - x\u00b2).\n\nSo integration by parts gives:\n\nuv - \u222b v du = x*(-sqrt(25 - x\u00b2)) - \u222b (-sqrt(25 - x\u00b2)) dx = -x sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) + \u222b sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) dx.\n\nNow, the original integral \u222b x\u00b2 / sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) dx = -x sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) + \u222b sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) dx.\n\nSo now, compute \u222b sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) dx. This is a standard integral, which equals (x/2) sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) + (25/2) arcsin(x/5) ) + C.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together:\n\n\u222b x\u00b2 / sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) dx = -x sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) + (x/2) sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) + (25/2) arcsin(x/5) ) + C\n\nSimplify the terms:\n\n- x sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) + (x/2) sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) = (-x + x/2) sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) = (-x/2) sqrt(25 - x\u00b2)\n\nSo the integral becomes:\n\n(-x/2) sqrt(25 - x\u00b2) + (25/2) arcsin(x/5) + C\n\nNow, evaluate this from 0 to 5/2.\n\nFirst at upper limit x=5/2:\n\nFirst term: (-5/4) sqrt(25 - (25/4)) = (-5/4) sqrt(75/4) = (-5/4) * (5*sqrt(3)/2) = (-25 sqrt(3))/8\n\nSecond term: (25/2) arcsin( (5/2)/5 ) = (25/2) arcsin(1/2) = (25/2)(\u03c0/6) = 25\u03c0/12\n\nAt lower limit x=0:\n\nFirst term: 0\n\nSecond term: (25/2) arcsin(0) = 0\n